WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:12.780
this chapter the troubleshooter the story of&nbsp;
Bernard Baruch a mighty explosion took place in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:21.780
July 1946 at bikini in the Pacific Ocean in that&nbsp;
mushrooming radioactive cloud the world faced a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:27.990
magnificent promise and a dreadful threat the&nbsp;
plan for the worldwide control of atomic energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:35.130
must be adopted by the United Nations otherwise&nbsp;
the atom bomb might boomerang and destroy us all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:43.050
the UN set up a commission to deal with the&nbsp;
problem the American delegate Bernard Baruch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:51.450
he wrote and presented the American proposals a&nbsp;
vast responsibility but for Baruch great tasks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.450 --> 00:01:00.960
were an old story for decades he had been the&nbsp;
troubleshooter of presidents Baruch was born in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:07.500
South Carolina five years after the Civil War as&nbsp;
a child he showed an uncanny knack for getting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:14.730
the heart of problems and solving them the family&nbsp;
moved to New York City in 1870 when the boy was 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:24.550
years old Barack's alma mater was the college&nbsp;
of the city of New York he was a good student

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.190
and so fine a boxer that he was advised to turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:33.730
professional but his southern&nbsp;
bred mother would not permit it

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:43.430
instead in 1890 he went to work in the New&nbsp;
York Stock Exchange Baruch learned quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:49.250
earned the respect of his elders and became&nbsp;
a partner in the brokerage firm in those days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:54.140
fortunes could be made or lost overnight&nbsp;
for Baruch it was the chance to become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.140 --> 00:02:00.710
wealthy he invested shrewdly in nineteen&nbsp;
five when he was 35 years old Baruch was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:10.990
worth millions of dollars but mere wealth left&nbsp;
you dissatisfied you searched for a higher goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:20.080
in 1917 you found that goal service to your&nbsp;
country President Woodrow Wilson signed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:25.660
declaration that brought the United States&nbsp;
into the first world war from a goldfish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:30.880
bowl were drawn the names of the first draft&nbsp;
teased an army of millions of men was being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:37.600
formed they needed uniforms guns ships all the&nbsp;
implements of warfare there was great confusion

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:46.970
you were given the task of organizing&nbsp;
American industry for war production

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:55.640
you brought industrial confusion to a halt did&nbsp;
your job so well the Germany later confessed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.640 --> 00:03:06.590
that American production played a major part in&nbsp;
her defeat the peace conference at Versailles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:13.250
you and Herbert Hoover were there with President&nbsp;
Wilson it was clear to you that Germany couldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:18.950
pay the huge war reparations demanded by&nbsp;
Britain and France you urged the delegates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:27.680
to take a more realistic approach for this you&nbsp;
were denounced by the Allies as pro-german with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:33.620
President Wilson you returned to the United&nbsp;
States disillusioned but determined to fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:42.890
by Wilson's side for the League of Nations&nbsp;
the fight was doomed to failure in 1920 the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:49.610
Republican Party swept back to power America&nbsp;
wanted to forget Europe Warren Harding became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:56.000
president although a lifelong Democrat Baruch&nbsp;
advised the new President on economic matters

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:07.050
Bernard Baruch continued to give councils and&nbsp;
Economic Affairs when Calvin Coolidge became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:16.230
president Herbert Hoover succeeded Coolidge as&nbsp;
the stock market climbed toward the sky no one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:21.510
would listen to burr Oaks warnings of impending&nbsp;
disaster suddenly the country was plunged into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:29.640
depression in the crisis Troubleshooters were&nbsp;
needed to find a way out men like Bernard Baruch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:42.120
1932 the Democratic Party convention Baruch and&nbsp;
Will Rogers were there burl felt a man strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:48.030
enough to lead the country out of depression was&nbsp;
Franklin Roosevelt he paid for several of FDR's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:58.350
election campaign broadcasts next year baroque&nbsp;
was present at Roosevelt's inauguration let me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:08.850
assert my firm belief that the only thing&nbsp;
we have to fear is fear itself the National&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:15.090
Recovery Act was launched with stirring big&nbsp;
city parades Farooq's close friend general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:21.090
Hugh Johnson was director of the NRA farooq&nbsp;
himself as a member of Roosevelt's inner circle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:30.440
when roosevelt's first cabinet assembled Beru&nbsp;
could have been among its members he had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:36.710
offered the post of Secretary of Treasury and&nbsp;
turned it down baroque didn't want public office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:44.540
he wanted only the chance to be of service&nbsp;
without glamour of publicity then Franklin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:52.100
Roosevelt took a step that temporarily disrupted&nbsp;
their friendship burr Luke disapproved several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:57.860
of the president's early fiscal measures he was&nbsp;
out of favor and four years was rarely called at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:05.330
the White House but with the Second World War&nbsp;
you went back into harness a troubleshooter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:11.780
was needed to solve the grave robber shortage and&nbsp;
Roosevelt chose you for the job as assistants you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:18.800
had James Conant of Harvard and Karl Compton of&nbsp;
Massachusetts Institute of Technology the problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:25.160
how to provide rubber for the Armed Forces and for&nbsp;
civilian needs out of America's small stockpile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:34.130
you studied the problem and reported to the White&nbsp;
House with recommendations the recommendations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:43.310
were approved and you reported to the people we&nbsp;
are recommending as the only means we have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.310 --> 00:06:56.810
able to see nationwide gasoline rationing but&nbsp;
for the purpose of conserving tires not gas our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.810 --> 00:07:05.990
choice is to cut discomfort or defeat new tires&nbsp;
for army trucks and artillery born of worn-out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:14.540
carcasses salvaged rubber synthetic rubber gas&nbsp;
rationing step by step the shortage was overcome

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:23.110
and back to the White House to undertake a new&nbsp;
assignment the manpower shortage looking into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:28.780
the problem you found cause for grave concern&nbsp;
with war production booming and millions of men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.780 --> 00:07:35.290
in service competition for labor was fierce&nbsp;
you found the solution a manpower budget

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:47.240
the unnecessary hoarding of workers&nbsp;
by manufacturers with cost plus war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:52.850
contracts should be eliminated employers should&nbsp;
be limited in the number of workers they could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:59.270
hire and workers should be limited in their&nbsp;
choice of jobs labor should be funneled into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:05.660
the war production industries where men were&nbsp;
needed desperately yet with a manpower budget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:13.280
civilian production industries would not suffer&nbsp;
unduly there would be enough workers for all

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:23.500
that task finished you visited the war&nbsp;
France in 1944 General George Patton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:28.540
gave you good news German resistance was&nbsp;
breaking they couldn't fight on much longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:38.050
but bad news when you returned home Franklin&nbsp;
Roosevelt had reached a limit of his strength

00:08:44.170 --> 00:08:51.310
the commander-in-chief passed away in April&nbsp;
1945 a few weeks before Germany surrendered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:56.650
it was a staggering loss to the nation&nbsp;
and a tremendous personal loss to you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.650 --> 00:09:02.200
in spite of disagreements you had admired&nbsp;
Franklin Roosevelt he'd been a great leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:15.680
Harry Truman stepped into the vacant place&nbsp;
and sent a plane on a mission that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:20.720
change the course of mankind's destiny&nbsp;
the bombers target was the Japanese city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:29.570
of Hiroshima or better or worse men had&nbsp;
unleashed a frightful power of the atom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.570 --> 00:09:36.470
Pandora's box was open the mushrooming cloud&nbsp;
assured in the age of atomic energy what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:44.480
bomb could do was written plain in the&nbsp;
city below death and desolation a city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:50.540
transformed into a heap of radioactive rubble&nbsp;
it happened once again before Japan surrendered

00:09:52.660 --> 00:10:00.970
now a new White House assignment for Bernard&nbsp;
Baruch to present America's atomic energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:07.990
control plan to the United Nations he called&nbsp;
for an international authority with powers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.990 --> 00:10:13.390
of inspection and Swift punishment for&nbsp;
violators of the agreement the Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.390 --> 00:10:18.730
delegate Andrei Gromyko objected the plan he&nbsp;
said was not acceptable to the Soviet Union&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:27.160
when the showdown came the smaller nations&nbsp;
waver but in the final vote they stood with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:33.310
the United States Russia would veto the plan&nbsp;
but the principles of atomic energy control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:39.310
advanced by baruch had been established it was&nbsp;
the crowning achievement of his eventful life

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:51.640
1953 a new president was in the White House&nbsp;
Dwight Eisenhower and Baruch renewed an old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:57.430
Association when Winston Churchill visited&nbsp;
the United States verruckt friendship with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.430 --> 00:11:03.070
Churchill dated back to the first world war&nbsp;
his friendship with Eisenhower began in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:11.460
second well past his 80th year Bernard Baruch&nbsp;
was still in harness he wanted it that way in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:18.000
service to others he had found the secret of human&nbsp;
happiness he had given much of his wealth to aid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:26.310
youngsters much of his strength to aid presidents&nbsp;
Bernard Baruch America's ace troubleshooter

